# Copy to .env.local if you need any of these. None are required.
#
# The installed Windows app sets what it needs for itself, from electron/main.cjs. Everything
# here exists for working in the repository, or for opening a backup by hand.
#
# Never commit a filled-in copy - .gitignore covers .env.* apart from this file.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ where things are
# Unset: the app writes to data/ beside the code, and reads db/ and templates/ from the
# working directory. That is what every verification script runs against.
#
# The installed app sets both: PNHS_DATA_DIR to %LOCALAPPDATA%\PNHS Records, and
# PNHS_APP_DIR to the unpacked bundle. See lib/paths.ts for why the data folder is in
# Local AppData and not Roaming, and why it must never sit inside OneDrive.
# PNHS_DATA_DIR=
# PNHS_APP_DIR=

# Overrides the database file alone, leaving originals where they are. The tests use it to
# reach a scratch file. You need it to open a backup:
#   $env:PNHS_DB_PATH = 'C:\backups\pnhs-backup-2026-08-18-1030\pnhs.db'; npm run dev
# PNHS_DB_PATH=

# The name of the data folder inside PNHS_DATA_DIR's parent, when PNHS_DATA_DIR is unset.
# It is read from the environment for a specific reason - see the note in lib/paths.ts - and
# there is almost never a reason to set it.
# PNHS_DATA_FOLDER=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ what is gone
# TURSO_DATABASE_URL / TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN and the four R2_* values were the hosted deployment:
# a libSQL database on Turso and imported originals in a Cloudflare R2 bucket. The app is a
# local Windows application now. The database is a file on the machine and the originals are
# a folder beside it, so there are no credentials left to leak.
#
# If you are looking at an old .env.local holding those values: the credentials in it are
# still live until somebody revokes them at the provider. Revoking them is the job, not
# deleting the file.
